contentText:
Bob Weir , guitarist and co-founder of&nbsp; The Grateful Dead , is being remembered by musicians, artists and public figures across the music and entertainment worlds following news of his death. He was 78.

Weir&rsquo;s death was confirmed Saturday (Jan. 10) by a statement published on his official social media accounts, where it was revealed he &ldquo;transitioned peacefully, surrounded by loved ones,&rdquo; after previously beating cancer and later succumbing to underlying lung issues.

Tributes began pouring in on Saturday after Weir&rsquo;s family confirmed his passing, describing him as having &ldquo;transitioned peacefully, surrounded by loved ones,&rdquo; after previously beating cancer and later succumbing to underlying lung issues.

Bravo host Andy Cohen shared an Instagram post on January 10, calling Weir &ldquo;That Guy&rdquo; for his &ldquo;impossibly beautiful and wildly fiery, intense and passionate&rdquo; presence.

He recalled his first Grateful Dead show in 1986 at Alpine Valley, Wisconsin, praising Weir&rsquo;s fierce voice as &ldquo;rays of sunshine,&rdquo; and fondly remembered Weir&rsquo;s appearances on Watch What Happens Live , including his graceful phrasing of Jerry Garcia&rsquo;s death as &ldquo;checkin&rsquo; out.&rdquo;

Don Felder, former Eagles guitarist, remembered first seeing Weir perform with the Grateful Dead at Woodstock.

&ldquo;I was blown away by that whole band, and the musicianship,&rdquo; Felder wrote, adding that he felt &ldquo;so blessed&rdquo; to have Weir sing on his solo track &ldquo;Rock You.&rdquo; Felder concluded his message with, &ldquo;Until we meet again, amigo.&rdquo;

Producer and bassist Don Was, who played with Weir in Wolf Bros for the past seven years, called the experience &ldquo;one of the most meaningful and rewarding experiences of my life.&rdquo;

Heart guitarist Nancy Wilson said the world would be a &ldquo;sadder place&rdquo; without Weir, writing, &ldquo;He spread a lifetime of magic around&hellip; His good vibrations will never end.&rdquo;

Primus frontman Les Claypool called Weir &ldquo;one of the nicest humans I&rsquo;ve ever met in the music world,&rdquo; describing him as &ldquo;a legend on many levels.&rdquo;

Michael Franti, in a lengthy statement, recalled Weir&rsquo;s openness and curiosity, sharing memories of performing together at a peace concert following the events of Sept. 11. &ldquo;A true giant in music made time to stand in a park with a few hundred people and help us send a musical message of peace,&rdquo; Franti wrote.

Additional tributes came from Sean Ono Lennon, who wrote, &ldquo;It was a great pleasure and a privilege to know you, brother,&rdquo; and Lee Ranaldo, who shared lyrics from the Grateful Dead&rsquo;s &ldquo;Sugar Magnolia,&rdquo; ending his message with, &ldquo;Thank you, Bob.&rdquo;
